		{
			"id": "eVbwU9PXZl3PRxoL7",
			"description": "Chainsword, Astartes Pattern",
			"local_notes": "Same rules as the human chainsword above (chain-vs-armour, jamming); scaled to a Marine's grip and strength.\n\nLore: The Adeptus Astartes issue a larger, heavier-toothed chainsword built to Astartes proportions - a recurring sidearm for Tactical and Assault Marines throughout Black Library fiction, distinct from (and more common than) power swords, which are reserved for veterans and officers.\n\nDesign: The sw+2d(2) cut formula is unchanged from the human-scale anchor: GCS resolves \"sw\" from the wielder's own ST, so an Astartes (ST 30, sw 5d+2) swinging this weapon already rolls 7d+2(2), average 26.5, against a Guardsman's 3d, without the weapon's damage line changing. Against flak (eff. DR 15) that penetrates 99% of hits and against carapace (DR 70, eff. 35) 2.2%, and it still skids off power armour either way: the chain-vs-armour gap the framework describes, compressed toward \"very occasionally\" for carapace. What must change, per the framework's instruction that human- and Astartes-scale versions need different min ST and weight, is the housing: heavier chain loop and motor (14 lb vs. 6 lb) and a min ST (15) far above what an unaugmented human can bring to bear one-handed, so a human could not wield this pattern even though the damage formula looks identical on paper. LC1 (Astartes-issue), not LC0, since the framework's LC1 bracket covers Astartes- carried war-gear one tier below power-armour-integrated items. Handling: parry 0 (Broadsword, as the human pattern). This is the anchor case for the project's \"same formula, heavier housing\" rule that the Astartes power sword and power axe below also follow. Monoedge rule (user direction): armour divisor (2) to (3). At ST 30 (7d+2(3)) it bites carapace on 74% of hits, a Mark VII neck seal on 86% and a Mark VII torso on 4%.",
			"tags": [
				"Melee Weapon",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "1",
