		{
			"id": "ed4fE40GZsmZqiqGS",
			"description": "Power Lance",
			"local_notes": "The full mounted-charge bonus (marked *) requires a mount at a gallop per the lance rules a GM is already using for cavalry combat; used from the ground it only reaches the braced profile. The U means the wielder cannot parry again with it until a turn has passed since the last parry - Low- Tech treats a couched lance the same way.\n\nLore: Carried by officers of Rough Rider-type Guard cavalry as an upgrade over the mundane hunting lance. In the fiction the Rough Riders' ordinary lance is an explosive-tipped hunting lance, not a power-field weapon; the power lance is an officer's weapon. Depicted as punching through infantry and light vehicles alike on a charge.\n\nDesign: Baseline mundane lance follows Basic Set cavalry lance logic (thr+3 imp mounted at a charge, extra reach only while mounted and moving); the power-field rule adds its usual +3d and Armor Divisor (10) on top. The braced (dismounted) line drops one point of charge momentum but keeps the field's own +3d bonus, since the field itself doesn't care whether the wielder is moving. Handling: re-derived against Low-Tech's real cavalry lance (a light wooden or metal-shod shaft, typically 8-10 lb, ST 11, parry -1U) - weight trimmed from 15 lb to 12 lb to sit close to that real analogue plus a little extra for the power cell and disruption emitter running the length of the shaft, with min ST unchanged at 11 (already matching the Low-Tech figure). Parry corrected to -1U on all three usages: Low-Tech already treats a couched or braced lance as unbalanced and slow to recover, which the previous flat -1 omitted.",
			"tags": [
				"Melee Weapon",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
